1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,301
2,000-plus PRs in a month,

2
00:00:01,321 --> 00:00:03,190
and she says she didn't review them one by one.

3
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:04,574
Lauren Tan works

4
00:00:04,594 --> 00:00:06,790
on Grok Bot at SpaceXAI.

5
00:00:06,810 --> 00:00:08,445
On October 2, Matt Pocock

6
00:00:08,465 --> 00:00:10,720
had her on his livestream for 65 minutes.

7
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:12,860
At 2,000 PRs, that's 67 a day.

8
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:15,490
At 15 minutes each, that's 17 hours.

9
00:00:15,510 --> 00:00:18,293
One person can't keep up, so she samples instead.

10
00:00:18,313 --> 00:00:20,970
Two things hold it together: verification and rules.

11
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:23,356
Verification: the AI uses the program

12
00:00:23,376 --> 00:00:24,970
like a real user.

13
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:27,602
Rules: a mistake the AI made once becomes a lint rule,

14
00:00:27,622 --> 00:00:29,230
so it can't happen again.

15
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:32,340
She wrote the method up as a set of skills called pstack.

16
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:34,410
In its official docs' full-autopilot mode,

17
00:00:34,430 --> 00:00:36,265
every PR gets a swarm of verifiers,

18
00:00:36,285 --> 00:00:38,390
and only a clean verdict lets it merge.

19
00:00:38,410 --> 00:00:40,993
She reviews after the merge: the morning commit log,

20
00:00:41,013 --> 00:00:43,230
revert what's wrong, add a rule.

21
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:45,462
But she says getting here is very hard,

22
00:00:45,482 --> 00:00:47,098
and for work that can't be undone or verified,

23
00:00:47,118 --> 00:00:48,540
she has no answer.

24
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:49,740
Review didn't vanish.

25
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,122
It moved from the door of every PR

26
00:00:51,142 --> 00:00:53,390
to the morning's commit log.
